&lt;h1 class="relative group">The Nature, Power, and Limits of Systems
&lt;div id="the-nature-power-and-limits-of-systems" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#the-nature-power-and-limits-of-systems" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>To do more than one person can do alone, people create systems.&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 class="relative group">A system turns resources into power
&lt;div id="a-system-turns-resources-into-power" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#a-system-turns-resources-into-power" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>A family, company, political party, government, military, religious organization, university, hospital, cooperative, social movement, or village community can all become a system. Each system gathers resources: money, people, knowledge, land, technology, authority, reputation, infrastructure, relationships, and experience.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>